auth {
  mode: none
}

headers {
  x-user-email: {{ownerEmail}}
}

docs {
  # vinylTrack API

  Exercises the API over HTTP against a running stack — the thing the
  in-process supertest suite structurally cannot do (it imports `app.ts`
  directly and never boots a listener).

  ## Running

  Start the stack and seed it, then:

      docker compose up -d
      docker compose run --rm seed
      cd be && npm run test:api

  Or open this folder in the Bruno app and pick the `local` environment.

  ## Identities

  Caller identity is the `x-user-email` dev header (#26), defaulted here at
  the collection level and overridden per request. The seeded users are:

  | var             | user                | access                                  |
  |-----------------|---------------------|-----------------------------------------|
  | `ownerEmail`    | kevin@example.com   | **not an admin**; owns both collections |
  | `adminEmail`    | admin@example.com   | admin; owns nothing of their own        |
  | `sharedEmail`   | alex@example.com    | shared on Squakville Vinyl only         |
  | `outsiderEmail` | jamie@example.com   | owns nothing, shared nothing            |
  | `unknownEmail`  | nobody@example.com  | not a user at all — expects 401         |

  Owner and admin are deliberately **separate** identities. If they were the
  same user, every request labelled "owner" would silently be taking the admin
  bypass in `policy.ts`, and the plain non-admin owner — what most family
  members actually are — would never be exercised at all. `ownerEmail` is the
  default; `adminEmail` is used only where admin behaviour is the point.

  ## Failure behaviour

  `npm run test:api` passes `--bail`, so the run stops at the first failure
  rather than cascading: a missing id in one response otherwise leaves a
  runtime var unresolved and fails every request after it, burying the real
  cause. The trade-off is that a bailed run skips `cleanup`, leaving `_BRUNO_`
  rows behind — re-seed to recover.

  ## Ordering

  Requests run in `seq` order and chain ids through runtime vars, so the
  folders are ordered by dependency: collection -> artist -> location ->
  album -> copy -> want -> cleanup. The `cleanup` folder removes everything
  the run created, so repeated runs stay idempotent.
}
